For $u\in\Omega$, there is a quaternion algebra $Q$ over $F$ with an embedding $Q\subset C_D(F)$ such that $$J_0F\subset Q\cap C_s,\qquad z\in Q,\qquad\mathop{\mathrm{Nrd}}_Q(z)=h.$$ The involution $*$ on $Q$ is its canonical quaternion conjugation. The algebra $Q$ is split at any field factor at which $-h(1-h)$ is a norm from $J_0F$.*



*Suppose that $a\in(J_0F)^\times$ satisfies $$\label{ind:a-norms}
 N_{J_0F/F}(a)=h,\qquad N_{J_0F/J_0}(a)=d_0.$$ Then $$\label{ind:two-factors}
 u=wq,\qquad w=za^{-1}\in\mathop{\mathrm{SL}}_1(Q),\qquad
 q=a(p^*)^{-1}\in\mathop{\mathrm{SU}}(C_s).$$ If only the first equation in <a href="#ind:a-norms" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="ind:a-norms">[ind:a-norms]</a> holds, the same factorization has $q\in\mathop{\mathrm{U}}(C_s)\cap S$.
